      "writtenOn": "2025-08-11T21:03:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Are the toggle types mutually exclusive or not? From the logs that you have provided, it looks like they both can be used. Then what happens if first we receive a notification that privacy was enabled on \"software\" toggle but then we receive a notification that privacy was disabled on \"hardware\" toggle? With the current code this will turn privacy off. However looking at the logic here:\n\nhttps://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/input/KeyboardLedController.java;l\u003d110;drc\u003d7bd2af8d8fa0280ca44738212f0a5b76b1583cc7\n\nand here:\n\nhttps://cs.android.com/android/platform/superproject/main/+/main:frameworks/base/services/core/java/com/android/server/sensorprivacy/SensorPrivacyService.java;l\u003d1282;drc\u003d61197364367c9e404c7da6900658f1b16c42d0da\n\nit seems that if any of toggles is enabled, the privacy should be enabled.\n\nSo we probably need to make `mMicPrivacyEnabled` something like a bitfield where we have bits for each type of toggle.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1773,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1773,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "b8ba733c095a16023a79894d40ab1059e3e3ae55",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2b765361_4c6474ed",
        "filename": "services/audiopolicy/service/AudioPolicyService.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1773,
      "author": {
        "id": 4227719
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-11T22:54:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ack.\nThere\u0027re two ways to fix this:\n - is to introduce one more member var that records Mic state for different toggle type . New member is needed as the existing bool mMicPrivacyEnabled is initiated by a single binder call hence we don\u0027t have toggleType information we can\u0027t reuse it.\n - if we don\u0027t mind having an extra binder call but want to save a member variable .. then replace the bool with a uint_8, call the api with toggle type (twice, for sw/hw respectively) inside registerSelf() to initiate its value; Actually have a third option with extra binder calls by keep using bool type: inside callback onSensorPrivacyChanged we issue a binder call to get Mic sensor state as it does inside registerSelf. onSensorPrivacyChanged is a oneway api so probably the impact can be acceptable ...\nPlease suggest.",
      "parentUuid": "79cfad67_b6414bb0",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1773,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1773,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "b8ba733c095a16023a79894d40ab1059e3e3ae55",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "148b6c87_18c57b1d",
        "filename": "services/audiopolicy/service/AudioPolicyService.cpp",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 1773,
      "author": {
        "id": 1042638
      },
      "writtenOn": "2025-08-11T23:29:38Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e inside callback onSensorPrivacyChanged we issue a binder call to get Mic sensor state\n\nI would not recommend using this approach because the service takes locks both when issuing these notifications and when querying sensor statuses, and by creating nested call chains there is always a possibility to create a deadlock condition. Instead, I would recommend to query the sensor state for hardware and software toggles separately during the initialization.\n\nAs for the member variables, since we currently use atomic variables, I\u0027m concerned with extra memory fences which are issued whenever we read or write such a variable. Since the privacy service sends `onSensorPrivacyChanged` for each update individually, we can only save on synchronization by using `memory_order_release` when updating the variable. But for reading in `isSensorPrivacyEnabled`, since we are always interested in the values of all variables, we could save some synchronization efforts by avoiding issuing a fence for every read operation.\n\nI think, for the most understandable approach, it\u0027s better to use 3 separate non-atomic variables here and protect them with a lock.",
      "parentUuid": "2b765361_4c6474ed",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 1773,
        "startChar": 8,
        "endLine": 1773,
        "endChar": 27
      },
      "revId": "b8ba733c095a16023a79894d40ab1059e3e3ae55",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
